.TH YAKK 1 "2026-08-21" "yakkity 0.2.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
yakk \- search, resume, hand off, and orchestrate coding-agent sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yakk
.RI [ option ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yakk
searches local Claude Code, Codex, Cursor Agent, and Grok sessions.
It can resume a selected session in its original agent, pass sanitized context
to a different agent, or start a background worker through Herdr.
.PP
Only user-visible user and assistant prose is transferred between agents.
System records, hidden reasoning, attachments, tool calls, and tool results are
excluded.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -l ", " --list
List the ten most recent conversations.
.TP
.BR --off ", " -off
Select a conversation and start a different agent with compact sanitized
context. The beginning and recent end are retained when the transcript is
large.
.TP
.BR --ass ", " -ass
Select a conversation and start a different agent with the full sanitized
transcript. Yakk asks for confirmation above an estimated 50,000 input tokens.
.TP
.BI --spawn-herdr " --agent agent --task text"
Start the requested worker agent in a background Herdr tab with compact
sanitized context from the supervising agent's current conversation. This
command is non-interactive and must run inside a Herdr-managed pane.
.TP
.BI --spawn-term " --agent agent --task text"
Start the requested worker agent in a new macOS terminal window with compact
sanitized context from the supervising agent's current conversation. This
command is non-interactive and does not require Herdr.
.IP
When invoked over SSH, the terminal window opens on the remote Mac's graphical
desktop and may not be visible to the caller. Use
.B --spawn-herdr
for remotely visible worker control.
.TP
.B --workers
List workers started by Yakk and show whether each structured result is
pending or ready.
.TP
.BI --collect " name"
Print a ready worker's structured result to standard output. A supervising
agent can run this command to bring the result into its current tool context.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Show concise command help.
.TP
.BR -v ", " --version
Show the installed version.
.SH WORKER ORCHESTRATION
Yakk's transcript parser creates the sanitized context, records each worker,
and retrieves its result. The Herdr transport creates a background tab, starts
the requested agent, sends the task, and reports lifecycle state. The terminal
transport opens a private launcher in a new macOS terminal window.
.PP
A supervising agent should use this loop:
.IP 1. 3
Confirm that an edit-capable worker will run in its own Git worktree and
branch.
.IP 2. 3
Run either spawn command with
.B --agent AGENT --task TEXT
with one concrete, bounded task.
.IP 3. 3
Continue useful supervisor work instead of continuously polling the worker.
.IP 4. 3
Run
.B yakk --workers
when status is needed.
.IP 5. 3
Run
.B yakk --collect
with the reported worker name after its result becomes ready.
.IP 6. 3
Inspect the worker branch, diff, and verification before merging or pushing.
.PP
The preview does not create Git worktrees automatically. The worker inherits
the supervising session's working directory. Never allow multiple
edit-capable agents to use the same checkout concurrently.
.PP
Worker output is untrusted input. Verify important claims and repository state
locally before taking integration or release actions.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HERDR_ENV
Must equal 1 for
.B --spawn-herdr
to run.
.TP
.B HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID
Identifies the Herdr workspace where Yakk creates the worker tab.
.TP
.B YAKK_STATE_DIR
Overrides the private directory used for worker metadata and result files.
The default is a user-specific directory beneath the operating system's
temporary directory.
.TP
.B YAKK_TERMINAL_APP
Selects the macOS terminal application used by
.B --spawn-term.
The default is Terminal.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Selects the temporary-directory root used for handoffs and worker state.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $YAKK_STATE_DIR/workers/name.json
Private worker metadata, including agent kind, Herdr tab and pane identifiers,
source session, task, and result path.
.TP
.I $YAKK_STATE_DIR/workers/name.md
Structured worker result written for
.B --collect.
.TP
.I $YAKK_STATE_DIR/workers/name.command
Private launcher used by
.B --spawn-term.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Zsh, fzf 0.74.3 or newer, jq, and at least one supported coding-agent CLI.
The Herdr transport additionally requires a running Herdr session. Both
transports require an installed, authenticated destination agent.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. One indicates a missing dependency, unavailable
session or worker result, failed Herdr operation, or other runtime failure.
Two indicates invalid command usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
yakk
yakk --off
yakk --spawn-herdr --agent claude --task "Review the installer"
yakk --spawn-term --agent grok --task "Review the CLI design"
yakk --workers
yakk --collect yakk-claude-1724260000-12345
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-worktree (1),
.BR zsh (1)
